As suggested by @jjsjann123, this PR adds an extra error check for the Exact and AlmostExact graphs. Specifically, it makes sure that all IDs in the same group should have the same extent. IDs with symbolic extents are simply ignored. The check is executed only when `EnableOption::IdModelExtraValidation` is turned on, which is true for all the C++ tests.

The check indeed found a bug of the AlmostExact graph. Specifically, when a broadcast is merged, we are mapping a non-broadcast input and its output. This conceptually makes sense since merging with a broadcast shouldn't change the concrete extent.

```
if (auto merge = dynamic_cast<Merge*>(expr)) {
    if (merge->inner()->isBroadcast() || merge->inner()->extent()->isOneInt()) {
      mapped_ids.push_back({merge->outer(), merge->out()});
    }
```

However, there's this behavior that I haven't considered. Specifically, when a broadcast ID that has an extent larger than 1 is merged, the merge output is also expanded by the factor of the broadcast extent. 

```
  Split: bS2{1} by factor 4 -> bS3{1}, bS4{4}
  Merge: iS1{i0} and bS4{4} -> iS5{( i0 * 4 )}
```

In this case, the problem is the AlmostExact graph maps `iS1` and `iS5`, but they shouldn't be mapped.

Maybe we should not create non-size-one broadcast IDs like `bS4` from the beginning? Their extents don't make any sense, so maybe we should enforce all broadcast IDs all have an extent of one?

